---
import ToolPage from '@versal/ui/ToolPage.astro';

const title = "JSON to Python Dict";
const description = "Convert a JSON sample into a Python dict literal  — valid identifier keys stay bare, invalid keys get quoted, and true/false/null become True/False/None.";
const faqs = [
  {
    "question": "How do I convert JSON to Python?",
    "answer": "Paste a JSON sample into the input and click Generate. The tool outputs a Python dictionary literal. Boolean values become True/False, null becomes None, string keys are quoted only when they are not valid Python identifiers."
  },
  {
    "question": "Is the output valid Python?",
    "answer": "Yes. The output is valid Python 3 code using standard dict literals with bare identifiers for valid keys and quoted strings for keys that are not valid Python identifiers. It can be copied directly into your project."
  }
];
import ToolNav from '@versal/ui/ToolNav.astro';
import BaseLayout from '@versal/ui/BaseLayout';
---

<ToolPage
  site="codegen"
  title={title}
  description={description}
  canonical="https://jsonversal.com/codegen/tools/json-to-python/"
  current="/codegen/tools/json-to-python/"
  faqs={faqs}
  about={[
    {
      "heading": "Identifier rules and the reserved-word list",
      "body": [
        "A key is written bare only when it matches `^[A-Za-z_][A-Za-z0-9_]*$` and is not a Python keyword; everything else becomes a single-quoted string. So `name` and `_internal` stay bare while `retry-count`, `2fa`, `class` and `user.name` get quoted. Quoting a keyword is not a workaround — `{'class': 1}` is a perfectly ordinary dict and is what the output produces. The reserved-word set is the soft-keyword-complete version, including `match` and `case`, which are contextual in Python but reserved by this check.",
        "Values follow a separate path and are always quoted strings, with backslashes, single quotes, newlines, carriage returns, tabs, form feeds and the two Unicode line separators escaped. Nesting is indented four spaces per level, and empty containers collapse to `{}` and `[]` rather than being spread over three lines each."
      ]
    },
    {
      "heading": "The literal mapping, and where the number type shifts underneath it",
      "body": [
        "Three substitutions happen on every value: `true`, `false` and `null` become `True`, `False` and `None`, at any depth. Numbers are emitted through JavaScript's own string conversion, and this is where the type of the literal can change. A JSON `1.0` has already lost its decimal point by the time it is a JavaScript number, so it is written as `1` and evaluates in Python as an `int` rather than a `float`. That is invisible in the output and shows up later as a type surprise.",
        "Integers beyond 2^53 are a related problem with no good solution at this layer. The input was parsed by `JSON.parse`, so precision was already lost before the generator saw it — a 64-bit identifier such as `9007199254740993` arrives as `...992`. If exactness matters, the value has to stay a string in the source JSON."
      ]
    },
    {
      "heading": "Why a round trip is not guaranteed",
      "body": [
        "Parsing the output with `ast.literal_eval` gives you back the structure, and for most fixtures that is the whole job. It is not an identity transform, though. JSON has one array type and Python has two, so every array comes back as a `list` and there is nothing in the source that says whether a tuple was intended. Float-versus-int collapses as described above. And a non-identifier key round-trips correctly only because it was quoted, which is to say the round trip works by accident of the quoting rule rather than by any encoding of the original key.",
        "The top level must be a JSON object — an array or a bare scalar is rejected with a message asking for an object, which is the right call since the output is a dict literal and nothing else. Deeply nested payloads are the practical limit rather than a correctness one: indentation grows by four spaces per level, so a response four levels deep is already visually unwieldy in a test file. For fixtures, the output is meant to be read and edited by a human, and a generated fixture nobody reads is a fixture nobody updates."
      ]
    }
  ]}
>

  <section class="card mt-1">
    <h2 class="h2-card">Paste your JSON</h2>
    <label for="input" class="sr-only">Input</label>
    <textarea id="input" placeholder='{"name": "Alice", "api_key": "sk-123", "retry-count": 3, "enabled": true, "meta": null}'></textarea>
    <div style="margin-top:12px;display:flex;gap:8px;">
      <button class="primary" id="gen">Generate Python dict</button>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="card mt-2" id="result" style="display:none;">
    <strong>Output  — Python dict literal</strong>
    <pre id="out" class="mt-2"></pre>
    <div style="margin-top:8px;display:flex;gap:8px;">
      <button class="ghost" id="copy">Copy</button>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="card mt-2" id="error" style="display:none;"></section>

  <h2>How it works</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Parses the JSON with <code>JSON.parse</code></li>
    <li>Renders keys as bare identifiers when they are valid Python identifiers, double-quoted strings otherwise</li>
    <li>Maps <code>true</code>/<code>false</code>/<code>null</code> to <code>True</code>/<code>False</code>/<code>None</code> and single-quotes strings with escaping</li>
    <li>Indents nested dicts and lists with 4 spaces</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>About JSON to Python</h2>
  <p class="text-muted">JSON and Python dict literals look similar but differ in the details: <code>true</code>/<code>false</code>/<code>null</code> become <code>True</code>/<code>False</code>/<code>None</code>, and while JSON requires every key to be double-quoted, Python lets valid identifiers be written bare and quotes anything else  — like keys with hyphens or leading digits.</p>
  <p class="text-muted">Reach for this tool when you are writing a Python script that needs test fixtures or a hard-coded dictionary copied from an API response. Watch out for deeply nested payloads (the indentation grows fast), and for numbers: the JSON passes through JavaScript numbers, so very large integers or floats can lose precision.</p>
</ToolPage>

<style>
  #error { color: var(--danger); border-color: var(--danger); }
</style>

<script is:inline>
(function(){
  const $ = id => document.getElementById(id);

  const PY_KEYWORDS = new Set(['False','None','True','and','as','assert','async','await','break','class','continue','def','del','elif','else','except','finally','for','from','global','if','import','in','is','lambda','nonlocal','not','or','pass','raise','return','try','while','with','yield','match','case']);

  function pyStr(s) {
    return "'" + s
      .replace(/\\/g, '\\\\')
      .replace(/'/g, "\\'")
      .replace(/\n/g, '\\n')
      .replace(/\r/g, '\\r')
      .replace(/\t/g, '\\t')
      .replace(/\u0008/g, '\\b')
      .replace(/\f/g, '\\f')
      .replace(/\u2028/g, '\\u2028')
      .replace(/\u2029/g, '\\u2029') + "'";
  }

  function pyKey(k) {
    return /^[A-Za-z_][A-Za-z0-9_]*$/.test(k) && !PY_KEYWORDS.has(k) ? k : pyStr(k);
  }

  function py(v, indent) {
    const pad = ' '.repeat(indent);
    const padIn = ' '.repeat(indent + 4);
    if (v === null) return 'None';
    if (typeof v === 'boolean') return v ? 'True' : 'False';
    if (typeof v === 'number') return String(v);
    if (typeof v === 'string') return pyStr(v);
    if (Array.isArray(v)) {
      if (v.length === 0) return '[]';
      const items = v.map(x => padIn + py(x, indent + 4));
      return '[\n' + items.join(',\n') + '\n' + pad + ']';
    }
    if (typeof v === 'object') {
      const keys = Object.keys(v);
      if (keys.length === 0) return '{}';
      const items = keys.map(k => padIn + pyKey(k) + ': ' + py(v[k], indent + 4));
      return '{\n' + items.join(',\n') + '\n' + pad + '}';
    }
    return 'None';
  }

  function showError(msg) {
    const e = $('error');
    e.textContent = msg;
    e.style.display = 'block';
    $('result').style.display = 'none';
  }

  $('gen').addEventListener('click', () => {
    $('error').style.display = 'none';
    const raw = $('input').value.trim();
    if (!raw) { showError('Please paste some JSON first.'); return; }
    let parsed;
    try { parsed = JSON.parse(raw); }
    catch (err) { showError('Invalid JSON: ' + err.message); return; }
    if (parsed === null || typeof parsed !== 'object' || Array.isArray(parsed)) {
      showError('Please provide a JSON object at the top level, e.g. {"name": "Alice"}.');
      return;
    }
    const code = '# Python dict literal generated from JSON by codegen.jsonversal.com\n' + py(parsed, 0) + '\n';
    $('out').textContent = code;
    $('result').style.display = 'block';
  });

  $('copy').addEventListener('click', async () => {
    try { await navigator.clipboard.writeText($('out').textContent); $('copy').textContent = 'Copied!'; setTimeout(() => $('copy').textContent = 'Copy', 1200); } catch {}
  });

  $('input').addEventListener('input', () => { $('error').style.display = 'none'; });
})();
</script>
